//
// UART 16550 wrapper testplan.
//
// V1 is one smoke test with a fixed configuration. Its exact inputs, expected
// values and pass/fail rules are in doc/dv/README.md. V2 and V3 testpoints list
// later work only; they are not implemented, so they map to no test yet.
//
// Sources: TI PC16550D datasheet SNLS378C (Rev C, May 2015), modified by the
// documented exceptions in README.md and doc/registers.md of this IP.
{
  name: "uart16550"

  testpoints: [
    ////////
    // V1 //
    ////////
    {
      name: smoke_reset_readback
      desc: '''
            After reset, read each register that has a documented reset value and check the full
            32-bit read data and that no access returns an error.

            Expected (datasheet Table 3, adjusted by the wrapper exceptions in doc/registers.md):
            IER 0x00, IIR 0x01, LCR 0x13, MCR 0x00, LSR 0x60, MSR 0x00.
            LCR is 0x13 rather than 0x00 because the wrapper reads WLS as 0b11 and resets EPS to 1.
            MSR is 0x00 because the modem inputs read as 0 when loopback is disabled.

            Also check that the serial output is high (idle) after reset (datasheet Table 3).
            SCR, DLL and DLM have no documented reset value, so only write/readback is checked.
            '''
      stage: V1
      tests: ["uart16550_smoke"]
    }
    {
      name: smoke_config
      desc: '''
            Configure the single selected smoke mode: 50 MHz clock, about 1 Mbaud, 8 data bits,
            no parity, 1 stop bit (8N1), polled operation with IER = 0. The wrapper also supports
            parity; other line settings are in the V2 line_config testpoint.

            - Write LCR = 0x83 (DLAB = 1, 8N1) and read back 0x83.
            - Write DLL = 0x03 and DLM = 0x00 and read both back. The wrapper keeps these values
              but always programs 1 Mbaud at 50 MHz; the first DLL/DLM write enables TX and RX.
            - Write LCR = 0x03 (DLAB = 0) and read back 0x03.
            - Write FCR = 0x07 (FIFO enable, clear both FIFOs, RCVR trigger level 1 byte).
            - Write SCR = 0xA5 and read back 0xA5.
            '''
      stage: V1
      tests: ["uart16550_smoke"]
    }
    {
      name: smoke_tx
      desc: '''
            Write 4 bytes to THR: 0x55, 0x01, 0x80, 0x00. Decode the serial output as 8N1 frames
            (start bit 0, data LSB first, stop bit 1) and compare them in order.

            - Exactly 4 frames must be seen. A wrong byte, a bad start or stop bit, a frame seen
              with nothing expected, or a missing frame at the deadline is an error.
            - After the last frame, LSR must reach 0x60 (THRE and TEMT set) within a bounded wait.
            '''
      stage: V1
      tests: ["uart16550_smoke"]
    }
    {
      name: smoke_rx
      desc: '''
            Drive 4 frames into the serial input at 1 Mbaud, 8N1: 0xAA, 0x02, 0x40, 0x7E.
            For each byte, poll LSR until DR is set (bounded), check on every poll read that OE,
            PE, FE, BI and LSR[7] are clear (an LSR read clears them, datasheet Section 8.6.3),
            read RBR and compare in order.

            0x00 is not used because the wrapper returns 0 when RBR is read with an empty FIFO.
            The RX bytes differ from the TX bytes so a TX/RX mix-up is detected.
            '''
      stage: V1
      tests: ["uart16550_smoke"]
    }
    {
      name: smoke_end_checks
      desc: '''
            At the end of the test:
            - exactly 4 TX frames were seen and the expected TX queue is empty;
            - exactly 4 RX bytes were read from RBR and checked (RX bytes are checked one at a
              time against a fixed list, so there is no RX queue);
            - no further TX frame appears during a quiet window of 2 character times;
            - LSR reads 0x60 (no received data, no errors, transmitter empty);
            - intr_tx_watermark_o, intr_tx_done_o and intr_rx_timeout_o stayed low for the whole
              test (the wrapper README says these are never enabled);
            - the alert channel finished its documented initialization handshake within a bounded
              time after reset (invalid differential pairs are expected only during that
              handshake), and afterwards all three alert pairs stayed correctly encoded and no
              alert was sent (OpenTitan alert handler theory of operation, see doc/dv/README.md);
            - no UVM_ERROR or UVM_FATAL was reported; any error must make the run fail.
            '''
      stage: V1
      tests: ["uart16550_smoke"]
    }

    ////////
    // V2 //
    ////////
    {
      name: csr_reset_and_rw
      desc: '''
            All registers: reset values, write/readback, read-only and write-only behaviour, upper
            byte writes ignored and reads 0, DLAB banking of offsets 0x0 and 0x4, IIR/FCR and
            RBR/THR aliases, address wrap every 32 bytes.
            '''
      stage: V2
      tests: []
    }
    {
      name: tl_errors
      desc: '''
            Non-zero a_address[1:0] returns an error. Documented gaps (integrity not checked,
            PutPartialData and size != 2) are recorded but not used as checks until implemented.
            '''
      stage: V2
      tests: []
    }
    {
      name: interrupts
      desc: '''
            IER enables, IIR identification and priority, and the interrupt outputs, including the
            documented IIR exceptions. Expected behaviour must be pinned to the RTL revision under
            test because PR #13 / issue #9 change IIR timeout behaviour.
            '''
      stage: V2
      tests: []
    }
    {
      name: fifo_levels
      desc: '''
            TX FIFO 32 bytes and RX FIFO 64 bytes: full, writes to a full XMIT FIFO dropped,
            RCVR overrun (LSR.OE), FCR FIFO reset, trigger levels.
            '''
      stage: V2
      tests: []
    }
    {
      name: rx_errors
      desc: '''
            Parity error, framing error and break detection, with the reporting timing in the
            doc/registers.md LSR row, and LSR[7]. Characters with a framing or parity error are
            not pushed into the RCVR FIFO (README.md, Functional differences).

            LSR[7] covers PE, FE and BI only, not OE (doc/registers.md LSR row: LSR[7] is the OR
            of PE, FE and BI; datasheet Section 8.6.3, bit 7).
            - After a PE, FE or BI error, read LSR: that error bit and LSR[7] are set.
            - After an isolated overrun (no PE, FE or BI pending), read LSR: OE is set and
              LSR[7] is 0.
            Clear-on-read (datasheet Section 8.6.3): OE, PE, FE and BI are cleared when LSR is
            read, and LSR[7] is cleared by an LSR read when no further error remains. Read LSR a
            second time with no new error in between: OE, PE, FE, BI and LSR[7] are all 0.
            '''
      stage: V2
      tests: []
    }
    {
      name: line_config
      desc: '''
            Parity enable and even/odd select, ignored WLS/STB/stick parity writes, TX break
            (LCR[6]), and loopback (MCR.LOOP) with the related MSR bits.
            '''
      stage: V2
      tests: []
    }
    {
      name: alerts
      desc: '''
            Directed alert fault injection and ping handshakes for the fatal_fault alert. Passive
            alert protocol checking is already part of V1 (smoke_end_checks). The 16550 map has
            no ALERT_TEST register, so a generic register-based alert test needs adapting rather
            than reusing as is.
            '''
      stage: V2
      tests: []
    }

    ////////
    // V3 //
    ////////
    {
      name: stress_and_reset
      desc: '''
            Random mixes of the V2 tests, reset during active transfers, baud tolerance of the
            receiver, and coverage closure.
            '''
      stage: V3
      tests: []
    }
  ]
}
